    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_64a777dbe52c67c113ed284914d4edc0fc3a20572eea500ee3b09b504a59e0ac",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source uses two complementary finite-representation chains rather than one literal succession. For the theta<=0 upper argument, a truncated exponential turns small-prime removal into a short Dirichlet polynomial, which can be multiplied by a finite zeta approximation and sampled uniformly. For the lower argument, a theta-dependent finite mollifier first cancels zeta uniformly off axis, and an Euler-product identity then replaces that mollifier by the exponential of an additive prime-power field, with the entire discrepancy isolated in the Omega(n)>nu_theta tail. Together these chains move between analytic, multiplicative, and stochastic representations while retaining high-probability leading-scale control.",
