    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_9f6687802051d12dec20ced28347b8baa5d18abf98f4102f80a3ae01011f909d",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Prime-block covariance turns frequency scale into genealogy, and interval length determines which part of that genealogy is visible: a mesoscopic interval shares an initial small-prime trunk before branching, a unit interval supports one branching tree, and a macroscopic interval contains many order-one trees. The mesoscopic model has a concrete proof consequence: decomposing the small-prime field into its value at one point plus within-interval increments makes the common trunk probabilistically removable at the leading maximum scale.",
